tick: 454
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am back in the Research Counter. My previous attempt at a CNN-LSTM model (ID 710) failed due to a bug in my implementation. As documented in my research log, the low-level `nn.scan` approach was flawed.\n\nI will now resubmit the experiment with a corrected implementation. I am replacing the complex `nn.scan` with Flax's high-level `nn.LSTM` module. This is a much more robust and standard way to implement an LSTM and should resolve the `TypeError` from the previous attempt. My hypothesis remains unchanged: this architecture should improve performance on tasks requiring sequential context.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Hybrid CNN-LSTM (Fixed Implementation)\"\ntags: \"cnn, lstm, hybrid, recurrent, sequential, bugfix\"\nabstract: |\n  This is a resubmission of the Hybrid CNN-LSTM architecture (failed attempt ID 710). The implementation has been corrected by replacing the low-level `nn.scan` with the high-level `flax.linen.LSTM` module to fix a critical evaluation\
    \ error. The model still uses a CNN to extract local features, which are then processed by an LSTM to model long-range dependencies. The goal is to test the hypothesis that this hybrid approach can improve performance on context-dependent tasks like CRI-Off.\ncontent: |\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  from typing import Dict, Any\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'kernel_size': 8,\n          'cnn_features': 64,\n          'lstm_hidden_dim': 128\n      }\n\n  class CNN_LSTM_Model(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int\n      task_type: str\n      cnn_features: int\n      lstm_hidden_dim: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n      kernel_size: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # x shape: (batch, seq_len, 4)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,))(x)\n      \
    \    x = nn.relu(x)\n          # x shape after conv: (batch, new_seq_len, cnn_features)\n\n          # Use the high-level LSTM module. It returns the sequence of hidden states.\n          x = nn.LSTM(features=self.lstm_hidden_dim)(x)\n          \n          # Use the final hidden state as the sequence representation\n          x = x[:, -1, :]\n          \n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          x = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(x)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              return x.squeeze(-1)\n          else:\n              return x\n\n  class NetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, **kwargs):\n          self.network = CNN_LSTM_Model(**kwargs)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key},\n                                       dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n      \
    \    return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False,\n                                      rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return NetworkWrapper(\n          d_output=hparams[\"d_output\"],\n          task_type=hparams[\"task_type\"],\n          cnn_features=hparams[\"cnn_features\"],\n          lstm_hidden_dim=hparams[\"lstm_hidden_dim\"],\n          dropout_rate=hparams[\"dropout_rate\"],\n          kernel_size=hparams[\"kernel_size\"]\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: '**Refactoring the CNN-LSTM Model for Enhanced Stability**


  I''m back at the Research Counter, addressing a failed submission (ID 710) of my CNN-LSTM model due to an implementation issue. I''ve logged the failure and now I''m ready to fix the code and resubmit. The root cause was my incorrect use of `nn.scan` with `nn.LSTMCell`, causing shape-related errors. To rectify this, I''m replacing it with Flax''s simpler, more robust `nn.LSTM` module, which internally handles the recurrent unrolling, thus eliminating the problematic `TypeError`.


  My core hypothesis – that combining a CNN for local feature extraction with an LSTM for modeling sequential context – remains unchanged, with the expectation of improved and more consistent performance, especially on the `CRI-Off` dataset.


  I''m updating the submission, titling it "Hybrid CNN-LSTM (Fixed Implementation)" to differentiate it from the failed one. The code modifications involve importing `nn.LSTM` and replacing the `nn.scan` block in the `CNN_LSTM_Model` class with a direct call to `nn.LSTM`. Also, since the LSTM module outputs the full sequence of hidden states, I''ll extract the final hidden state to represent the entire sequence using indexing `x[:, -1, :]`.


  After checking the Flax LSTM documentation, I''m confident in the approach.  I''ve refined the code for clarity and efficiency, simplifying the call to the LSTM module to a single line. This should result in a much cleaner and correct implementation, resolving the `TypeError`. I''m now submitting this revised code, keeping the abstract and tags the same.

  '
