tick: 462
role: model
parts:
- text: "My research log has been updated with the latest findings. My new hypothesis is that a model needs access to both global motif presence (via max pooling) and sequential context (via LSTM) to succeed across all diverse datasets.\n\nI will now implement this \"dual-pathway\" architecture and submit it for evaluation. I'll return to the Research Counter and submit the new experiment in a single turn.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"CNN with Parallel LSTM and Max-Pooling Aggregation\"\ntags: \"cnn, lstm, hybrid, max-pooling, parallel\"\nabstract: |\n  This model addresses the specialization issue observed in previous architectures (ID 701 & 729). After an initial CNN feature extraction layer, the model splits into two parallel pathways. One pathway uses global max-pooling to capture the presence of key motifs (strong for ncRNA). The other uses an LSTM to model sequential context (strong for CRI-Off). The outputs of both pathways are concatenated\
    \ before the final prediction layer, allowing the model to learn the optimal combination of features for each specific task.\ncontent: |\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  from typing import Dict, Any\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'kernel_size': 8,\n          'cnn_features': 64,\n          'lstm_hidden_dim': 64 # Halved, as it will be concatenated with pooling output\n      }\n\n  class ParallelCNN_LSTM_Model(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int\n      task_type: str\n      cnn_features: int\n      lstm_hidden_dim: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n      kernel_size: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # 1. CNN Feature Extraction\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,))(x)\n          cnn_output = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          # 2. Parallel Aggregation\
    \ Pathways\n          # Pathway A: Global Max Pooling (for motif presence)\n          pooled_output = jnp.max(cnn_output, axis=1)\n\n          # Pathway B: LSTM (for sequential context)\n          rnn_layer = nn.RNN(nn.OptimizedLSTMCell(features=self.lstm_hidden_dim))\n          rnn_sequence_output = rnn_layer(cnn_output)\n          lstm_output = rnn_sequence_output[:, -1, :] # Final hidden state\n\n          # 3. Concatenate Pathway Outputs\n          combined_output = jnp.concatenate([pooled_output, lstm_output], axis=-1)\n          \n          # 4. Final Prediction Head\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(combined_output, deterministic=deterministic)\n          x = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(x)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              return x.squeeze(-1)\n          else:\n              return x\n\n  class NetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, **kwargs):\n          self.network = ParallelCNN_LSTM_Model(**kwargs)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key,\
    \ dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key},\n                                       dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False,\n                                      rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return NetworkWrapper(\n          d_output=hparams[\"d_output\"],\n          task_type=hparams[\"task_type\"],\n          cnn_features=hparams[\"cnn_features\"],\n          lstm_hidden_dim=hparams[\"lstm_hidden_dim\"],\n          dropout_rate=hparams[\"\
    dropout_rate\"],\n          kernel_size=hparams[\"kernel_size\"]\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: "**Let's Get This Hybrid Model Out the Door!**\n\nOkay, the user wants me to execute this plan, so let's get moving. First things first: I'm in the Private Memory Room, and my primary goal is to implement and submit this new hybrid model – the one with those parallel aggregation pathways, Max Pooling and LSTM.  \n\nHere's the plan. I'll need to go back to the Research Counter, and then I can submit the code. That should be a single-turn operation.\n\n**The Execution Strategy:**\n\n1.  `/execute_action{goto research}`\n2.  `/execute_action{submit 1}` with the updated YAML and code.\n\n**Diving into the Code Implementation:**\n\nNow for the details.  The model is going to take the CNN output and split into those two key branches: \n*   **Branch A:** That's a simple `jnp.max(x, axis=1)` for the Global Max Pooling.\n*   **Branch B:** We're going with `nn.RNN(nn.OptimizedLSTMCell(...))(x)[:, -1, :]` for the LSTM, capturing that final hidden state.\n\nThen, we'll concatenate\
  \ the outputs of both: `jnp.concatenate([branch_a_out, branch_b_out], axis=-1)`. After that, it's just the usual dropout and dense layers.  The `_define_hyperparameters`, `NetworkWrapper`, all of that should stay the same.\n\n**Hyperparameter Tweaks:**\n\nI'll need to tweak the hyperparameters a bit. Since we're combining outputs, the `lstm_hidden_dim` and possibly even the `cnn_features` can be smaller. I'm thinking `cnn_features=64` and `lstm_hidden_dim=64` to keep the concatenated dimension at a reasonable 128. That feels like a good starting point to start the experimentation.  It seems like I have everything ready!\n"
