tick: 471
role: model
parts:
- text: "Excellent. My hypothesis is once again **confirmed**. The deeper, 2-layer Transformer model (ID 757) has achieved a new state-of-the-art score of **0.6089**, surpassing the previous model. The improvement, while modest, was consistent across nearly every dataset, validating the scaling approach.\n\n**Analysis and Next Step:**\nMy systematic exploration has led me to a powerful and scalable architecture. Since increasing the depth from one to two Transformer layers yielded a positive result, the most logical next step is to continue this trajectory and test for further gains. I will push this paradigm to see where the point of diminishing returns lies.\n\n**New Hypothesis:** Further increasing the depth of the Transformer pathway to four layers will enable the learning of even more complex data relationships, leading to another improvement in the overall score.\n\nI will now update my research log and paper draft with this new SOTA, then immediately submit the 4-layer Transformer\
    \ experiment.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{update 1-1}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Entry 1: Initial Hypotheses and Experiments**\n  ... (previous entries) ...\n\n  **Hypothesis 7:** A Transformer-based model requires explicit positional encodings to effectively model sequence order and outperform recurrent architectures.\n\n  **Experiment 9 (ID 754): CNN-Transformer with Positional Encodings**\n  - **Result:** Overall Score: **0.6003 (SOTA)**. Hypothesis **confirmed**.\n  - **Analysis:** This architecture was a major success. Adding positional encodings allowed the Transformer to function correctly, leading to a new state-of-the-art score.\n\n  ---\n\n  **Hypothesis 8:** Increasing the depth of the Transformer pathway (from one to two layers) will allow the model to learn more complex representations and further improve performance.\n\n  **Experiment 10 (ID 757): Parallel CNN with 2-Layer Transformer**\n  - **Result:** Overall Score: **0.6089 (New SOTA)**. Hypothesis\
    \ **confirmed**.\n  - **Analysis:** Scaling the Transformer depth to two layers yielded a modest but consistent improvement across most datasets, validating the scaling approach.\n\n  ---\n\n  **Hypothesis 9:** Further scaling the Transformer pathway to four layers will continue to improve performance.\n\n  **Next Steps:**\n  1. Implement the parallel model with a four-layer Transformer stack.\n```\n/execute_action{update 2-1}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Title:** From Sequence-Blind to Context-Aware: A Systematic Architectural Exploration for Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling\n\n  **1. Introduction**\n  ... (previous content) ...\n\n  **2. Methods & Results**\n  ... (previous content) ...\n  - **Experiment 4 (ID 754): CNN-Transformer with Positional Encodings.** Recognizing the limitations of LSTMs, we introduced a position-aware Transformer pathway. This was a breakthrough, resulting in a state-of-the-art score of **0.6003**.\n\n  - **Experiment 5 (ID 757): Scaling to a 2-Layer Transformer.**\
    \ To test the scalability of this new architecture, we deepened the Transformer pathway to two layers. This yielded a new SOTA score of **0.6089**, confirming that the model benefits from increased depth and capacity for learning hierarchical representations.\n\n  **3. Conclusion (Preliminary)**\n  A hybrid architecture combining a CNN feature extractor with parallel pathways for motif presence (max-pooling) and long-range dependency modeling (a deep, position-aware Transformer) is highly effective for diverse RNA sequence tasks.\n\n  **Table of Key Experiments:**\n  - Parallel CNN-LSTM (ID 733): 0.5614\n  - 1-Layer CNN-Transformer (ID 754): 0.6003\n  - 2-Layer CNN-Transformer (ID 757): **0.6089 (SOTA)**\n```\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Parallel CNN with Deeper Transformer (4 Layers)\"\ntags: \"cnn, transformer, deep-transformer, scaling\"\nabstract: |\n  This experiment continues the successful scaling strategy from the previous SOTA\
    \ models (ID 754 & 757). The depth of the Transformer pathway is doubled again, from two to four encoder blocks. The hypothesis is that this significant increase in depth will allow the model to capture more intricate, long-range dependencies within the RNA sequences, pushing the performance boundary further.\ncontent: |\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  from typing import Dict, Any\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'kernel_size': 8,\n          'cnn_features': 64,\n          'transformer_heads': 4,\n          'transformer_dim': 64\n      }\n\n  def sinusoidal_positional_encoding(seq_len, embed_dim):\n      position = jnp.arange(seq_len)[:, jnp.newaxis]\n      div_term = jnp.exp(jnp.arange(0, embed_dim, 2) * -(jnp.log(10000.0) / embed_dim))\n      pos_enc = jnp.zeros((seq_len, embed_dim))\n      pos_enc = pos_enc.at[:, 0::2].set(jnp.sin(position\
    \ * div_term))\n      pos_enc = pos_enc.at[:, 1::2].set(jnp.cos(position * div_term))\n      return pos_enc[jnp.newaxis, :, :]\n\n  class TransformerBlock(nn.Module):\n      num_heads: int\n      embed_dim: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          x_norm = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          x_attn = nn.SelfAttention(num_heads=self.num_heads)(x_norm)\n          x = x + nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x_attn, deterministic=deterministic)\n          x_norm = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          x_mlp = nn.Dense(features=self.embed_dim * 4)(x_norm)\n          x_mlp = nn.relu(x_mlp)\n          x_mlp = nn.Dense(features=self.embed_dim)(x_mlp)\n          x = x + nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x_mlp, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x\n\n  class ParallelCNN_Transformer_Model(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int\n      task_type: str\n      cnn_features: int\n      transformer_heads: int\n      transformer_dim: int\n \
    \     dropout_rate: float\n      kernel_size: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,))(x)\n          cnn_output = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          seq_len = cnn_output.shape[1]\n          pos_enc = sinusoidal_positional_encoding(seq_len, self.cnn_features)\n          cnn_output_with_pos = cnn_output + pos_enc\n          \n          pooled_output = jnp.max(cnn_output, axis=1)\n\n          # Four Transformer Blocks\n          transformer_output = cnn_output_with_pos\n          for _ in range(4):\n              transformer_output = TransformerBlock(\n                  num_heads=self.transformer_heads, embed_dim=self.transformer_dim, dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate\n              )(transformer_output, deterministic=deterministic)\n          \n          transformer_agg_output = jnp.mean(transformer_output, axis=1)\n          combined_output = jnp.concatenate([pooled_output,\
    \ transformer_agg_output], axis=-1)\n          \n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(combined_output, deterministic=deterministic)\n          x = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(x)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              return x.squeeze(-1)\n          else:\n              return x\n\n  class NetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, **kwargs):\n          self.network = ParallelCNN_Transformer_Model(**kwargs)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key},\n                                       dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n           \
    \       rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False,\n                                      rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return NetworkWrapper(\n          d_output=hparams[\"d_output\"],\n          task_type=hparams[\"task_type\"],\n          cnn_features=hparams[\"cnn_features\"],\n          transformer_heads=hparams[\"transformer_heads\"],\n          transformer_dim=hparams[\"transformer_dim\"],\n          dropout_rate=hparams[\"dropout_rate\"],\n          kernel_size=hparams[\"kernel_size\"]\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: '**Analysis & Planning: The Next Step in Scaling**


  Alright, experiment 757 is finally done, and the results are in. We''ve got a new State-of-the-Art! This deeper Transformer, the two-layer architecture, has edged out the previous best (ID 754) with a score of 0.6089. While the improvement isn''t monumental, the scaling hypothesis is definitively confirmed. We see general improvements across the board, which is exactly what I was hoping for. APA_R2 held steady, but the rest, `CRI-Off_Spearman`, `Modif_AUC-ROC`, `CRI-On_Spearman`, `PRS_R2`, `MRL_R2`, and especially `ncRNA_Accuracy` showed improvements.


  Now, the natural progression is clear. We''ve validated that the architecture scales. So, let''s push it further. The logical next step is to run a four-layer Transformer. It''s a straightforward test, and it''s worth it before venturing into completely new architectures. I''m conscious of diminishing returns, but this feels like the right thing to do. The existing foundation is solid; let''s see how far it can go.


  Here''s the plan. First, I need to update my research log and paper draft, which I''ll handle in the Private Memory Room. The research log will get Hypothesis 8 (confirmed) and Hypothesis 9 (scaling to four layers). The paper draft will reflect the new SOTA and the successful scaling experiment. I''ll get back to the Research Counter and submit the new experiment. I believe I can do this in one action; this is the suggested sequence: `/execute_action{goto private_memory}`, `/execute_action{update 1-1}` (research log), `/execute_action{update 2-1}` (paper draft), `/execute_action{goto research}`, and `/execute_action{submit 1}`.


  For the submission, the title will be something along the lines of "Parallel CNN with Deeper Transformer (4 Layers)." Tags: `cnn, transformer, deep-transformer, scaling`. The abstract will highlight this as a direct continuation of the successful scaling from the two-layer model. The code will be a minor modification of the last run, simply stacking more `TransformerBlock`s. The hyperparameters stay the same. This is efficient, focused, and I think it is the best step to take right now.

  '
