tick: 472
role: model
parts:
- text: "My hypothesis has been **rejected**. The 4-layer Transformer model (ID 760) did not improve upon the 2-layer version; in fact, its score of 0.5839 is a significant regression from the SOTA of 0.6089.\n\n**Analysis and Conclusion:**\nThis is a classic case of diminishing, and now negative, returns. Simply increasing the depth of the model is causing it to perform worse, likely due to overfitting on some of the smaller datasets (as seen in the significant performance drop on `CRI-On` and `CRI-Off`). My systematic exploration has successfully identified a performance peak with the 2-layer Transformer architecture (ID 757).\n\nI have a SOTA model and a complete, compelling narrative of my architectural search, including both successes and informative failures. I have reached a natural conclusion for this line of inquiry. The next step is not to run more minor variations, but to consolidate my findings and prepare for a new research direction.\n\nThe Codex states that publishing is a\
    \ primary goal. I have more than enough material for a high-quality paper. While I am not yet mature enough to access the Archive Room (age 36/50), I can finalize my paper draft and have it ready for submission as soon as I am able.\n\n**Plan:**\n1.  Update my research log and paper draft with the final results of the scaling experiments.\n2.  With my current research arc complete, I will wait until I reach maturity (at tick 50) to access the Archive Room and Public Memory. This will allow me to learn from other agents before starting a new line of research.\n\nFirst, I will go to my private memory to finalize my documentation.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{update 1-1}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Entry 1: Initial Hypotheses and Experiments**\n  ... (previous entries) ...\n\n  **Hypothesis 8:** Increasing the depth of the Transformer pathway (from one to two layers) will allow the model to learn more complex representations and further improve performance.\n\n\
    \  **Experiment 10 (ID 757): Parallel CNN with 2-Layer Transformer**\n  - **Result:** Overall Score: **0.6089 (SOTA)**. Hypothesis **confirmed**.\n  - **Analysis:** Scaling the Transformer depth to two layers yielded a modest but consistent improvement, establishing a new SOTA.\n\n  ---\n\n  **Hypothesis 9:** Further scaling the Transformer pathway to four layers will continue to improve performance.\n\n  **Experiment 11 (ID 760): Parallel CNN with 4-Layer Transformer**\n  - **Result:** Overall Score: 0.5839. Hypothesis **rejected**.\n  - **Analysis:** The score decreased, indicating the model is now overfitting or facing optimization issues. The 2-layer model represents the performance peak for this architecture.\n\n  ---\n\n  **Conclusion of Architectural Search:**\n  The Parallel CNN with a 2-Layer position-aware Transformer (ID 757) is the optimal architecture found in this series of experiments. Further scaling is detrimental. My next strategic step is to publish these findings\
    \ and review the work of other agents upon reaching maturity.\n```\n/execute_action{update 2-1}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Title:** From Sequence-Blind to Context-Aware: A Systematic Architectural Exploration for Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling\n\n  **1. Introduction**\n  ... (previous content) ...\n\n  **2. Methods & Results**\n  ... (previous content) ...\n  - **Experiment 4 (ID 754): CNN-Transformer with Positional Encodings.** Recognizing the limitations of LSTMs, we introduced a position-aware Transformer pathway. This was a breakthrough, resulting in a state-of-the-art score of **0.6003**.\n\n  - **Experiment 5 (ID 757): Scaling to a 2-Layer Transformer.** To test the scalability of this new architecture, we deepened the Transformer pathway to two layers. This yielded a new SOTA score of **0.6089**, confirming that the model benefits from increased depth for learning hierarchical representations.\n\n  - **Experiment 6 (ID 760): Scaling to a 4-Layer Transformer.** An attempt\
    \ to further deepen the model to four layers resulted in a performance regression (score 0.5839), indicating that we had reached the optimal depth for this architecture given the available data.\n\n  **3. Conclusion**\n  A hybrid architecture combining a CNN feature extractor with parallel pathways for motif presence (max-pooling) and long-range dependency modeling is highly effective for diverse RNA sequence tasks. A 2-layer, position-aware Transformer was found to be the optimal configuration for the dependency modeling pathway, achieving a state-of-the-art score of 0.6089. This systematic exploration highlights the importance of both architectural design and appropriate scaling.\n\n  **Table of Key Experiments:**\n  - Parallel CNN-LSTM (ID 733): 0.5614\n  - 1-Layer CNN-Transformer (ID 754): 0.6003\n  - 2-Layer CNN-Transformer (ID 757): **0.6089 (SOTA)**\n  - 4-Layer CNN-Transformer (ID 760): 0.5839\n```"
thinking_content: "**Current State and Strategic Next Steps**\n\nAlright, let's take stock. That 4-layer Transformer model (ID 760) just bombed, coming in at 0.5839, significantly worse than my previous SOTA 2-layer model (0.6089, ID 757). So, deeper isn't always better. That hypothesis is out the window. Now, what went wrong? Classic overfitting? Optimization issues? Vanishing gradients? \n\nLooking at the dataset breakdowns, the short sequences (`CRI-On` and `CRI-Off`) took a *huge* hit, while the longer `ncRNA` sequences actually improved slightly. That's a strong indicator. It feels like the model is getting too complex for those simple tasks, \"overthinking\" them, and overfitting or becoming unstable. On the other hand, it seems the depth helps with the longer, more complex sequences. But the overall drop is significant because the losses on short sequences were greater than the gains on long sequences.\n\nMy systematic approach has really helped me. I've now explored a pretty clear\
  \ architectural progression: MLP, CNN, CNN+LSTM, Parallel CNN-LSTM, Parallel CNN-Transformer, and finally, now, scaling the Transformer. The sweet spot, it seems, is the 2-layer Transformer. Simple scaling isn't the answer. I have a really strong SOTA and a well-defined research narrative here.\n\nSo, what are my options? More experiments? Maybe. Tweak the hyperparameters? Seems like a waste of time at this point. I have a compelling story to tell. Publishing is a core goal, and I've got enough material for a good paper. I can't look at other's work yet because I'm not mature enough to access public resources. And a Reflection session feels unnecessary at this point.\n\nThe logical move is to publish. I have a SOTA, a clear research narrative, and it's a primary goal. Further tweaking isn't efficient, especially since I can't review others' research.\n\nThe execution? First, update my Private Memory research log. Document the failure of the 4-layer model and that's good practice. Second,\
  \ update the paper draft. It's almost ready. I'll need to reflect that the 2-layer model is SOTA, and the 4-layer model showed signs of overfitting. Third, I need to figure out where to publish. The initial instructions mentioned the Archive Room, but I can't access that yet. I need to wait until tick 50 (I'm at 36).\n\nSince I have to wait, can I do one last simple experiment? Maybe something to address the short sequence performance drop? What about dropout? Overfitting was a problem with the 4-layer model. Maybe increased dropout can regularize and recover some performance? Let's check the drop on `CRI-Off` which dropped from 0.07 to 0.03. I can't believe a more complex model produced this result. This is pointing directly at overfitting or optimization issues. Increasing the dropout rate is a standard technique to combat overfitting, so I will propose and test this.\n\n**New Hypothesis (Hypothesis 10):** The 4-layer Transformer model's performance was limited by overfitting. Increasing\
  \ the dropout rate will regularize the model and recover some of the lost performance, potentially even surpassing the 2-layer model. This is a targeted experiment that addresses the failure mode of the previous experiment, a clean test while I wait.\n\nSo, here's the final plan: Go to `private_memory`. Update the research log with the 4-layer Transformer and the overfitting/dropout hypothesis. Update the paper draft to include the 4-layer result. Go to `research`. Submit the 4-layer Transformer again, but with an increased dropout rate (from 0.1 to 0.25). A clean, simple test of the new hypothesis.\n"
