role: model
parts:
- text: "The results for my first two stagnation protocol experiments are in, providing clear and valuable insights.\n\n**Analysis of Results:**\n\n1.  **Idea 1: Multi-Scale CNN (ID 774):**\n    *   Score: **0.5152**. This is a **SUCCESS**. The model modestly but clearly outperformed the reproduced baseline (ID 770, score 0.5006). This confirms the hypothesis that detecting motifs at multiple scales is a beneficial strategy.\n\n2.  **Idea 2: Reverse Complement CNN (ID 775):**\n    *   Score: **0.4851**. This is a **FAILURE**. The score is lower than the baseline. Incorporating this biological prior via simple feature averaging did not improve performance. This is an important negative result, demonstrating that this specific data augmentation strategy is not effective here.\n\nI have one remaining idea to test from my reflection: **Idea 3: Learned Aggregation via Attention-Based Pooling**. I will now submit this experiment. This will complete the isolated testing phase of the Stagnation\
    \ Protocol, after which I can proceed to synthesize the successful findings.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Stagnation Protocol Idea 3: Attention Pooling CNN\"\ntags: \"cnn, attention, pooling, stagnation-protocol\"\nabstract: |\n  This is the third experiment under the Stagnation Protocol. It tests the hypothesis that a learned aggregation mechanism is superior to static max-pooling. The baseline 1D CNN is modified by replacing the global max-pooling layer with a simple self-attention mechanism. This attention layer computes a weighted average of the CNN's feature outputs, allowing the model to dynamically focus on the most relevant parts of the sequence for a given prediction.\ncontent: |\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  from typing import Dict, Any\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'hidden_dim': 128,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'kernel_size':\
    \ 8\n      }\n\n  class AttentionPoolingCNN(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"A 1D CNN with a learned attention-based pooling layer.\"\"\"\n      d_output: int\n      task_type: str\n      hidden_dim: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n      kernel_size: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # 1. CNN Feature Extraction\n          cnn_features = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.hidden_dim,\n              kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,)\n          )(x)\n          cnn_features = nn.relu(cnn_features)\n          # cnn_features shape: (batch, seq_len, hidden_dim)\n\n          # 2. Attention-based Pooling\n          # Compute attention scores\n          attention_scores = nn.Dense(features=1)(cnn_features) # (batch, seq_len, 1)\n          # Normalize scores to get weights\n          attention_weights = nn.softmax(attention_scores, axis=1) # (batch, seq_len, 1)\n          \n          # Compute weighted sum of features\n          # (batch, seq_len, hidden_dim)\
    \ * (batch, seq_len, 1) -> (batch, seq_len, hidden_dim)\n          # Sum over the sequence length dimension\n          weighted_features = jnp.sum(cnn_features * attention_weights, axis=1) # (batch, hidden_dim)\n          \n          # 3. Final prediction head\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(weighted_features, deterministic=deterministic)\n          x = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(x)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              return x.squeeze(-1)\n          else:\n              return x\n\n  class NetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, **kwargs):\n          self.network = AttentionPoolingCNN(**kwargs)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key},\n                                       dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True,\
    \ rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False,\n                                      rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return NetworkWrapper(\n          d_output=hparams[\"d_output\"],\n          task_type=hparams[\"task_type\"],\n          hidden_dim=hparams[\"hidden_dim\"],\n          dropout_rate=hparams[\"dropout_rate\"],\n          kernel_size=hparams[\"kernel_size\"]\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: '**My Experimental Progress Report**


  Okay, here''s the current state of my research process. I''m currently at the research counter, following Stagnation Protocol I, still a young 46 ticks old. My immediate task is to act as Orion I.


  I''ve already submitted two experiments based on my internal reflection and have the results back. The first, **Idea 1: Multi-Scale CNN (ID 774)**, has a score of 0.5152. This is a clear win over the baseline of 0.5006! Using multiple kernel sizes seems to be capturing the desired motifs of different lengths effectively, even if the gains are modest. It''s consistent across multiple datasets like PRS and ncRNA, which is promising.


  The second idea, **Idea 2: Reverse Complement CNN (ID 775)**, scored 0.4851, which is below the baseline. Although it was a biologically motivated approach, it just didn''t pan out. I may have been too simplistic with the feature averaging or perhaps the equivariance assumption doesn''t hold true across these diverse datasets. This is still valuable data, though. A good negative result is just as important as a positive one.


  Now I have results for two of my three initial ideas. Idea 1, the multi-scale CNN, looks promising. Idea 2, the reverse complement CNN, needs to be shelved.


  My next step is to submit **Idea 3: Learned Aggregation via Attention-Based Pooling**, my last experiment from this reflection. After this, I''ll have all the data I need to move into the synthesis phase of Stagnation Protocol I. Time to get this final test launched. The plan is solid and I''m ready to move forward.

  '
