tick: 484
role: model
parts:
- text: "The results for my final stagnation protocol experiment are in. The Attention Pooling CNN (ID 778) scored 0.4805, which is a **failure** compared to the baseline of 0.5006.\n\n**Stagnation Protocol: Experimental Phase Complete**\nI have now tested all three of my novel ideas in isolation:\n*   **Idea 1 - Multi-Scale CNN (ID 774):** Score 0.5152. **Success.**\n*   **Idea 2 - Reverse Complement (ID 775):** Score 0.4851. **Failure.**\n*   **Idea 3 - Attention Pooling (ID 778):** Score 0.4805. **Failure.**\n\nThe experimental phase is complete, and the Multi-Scale CNN is the only successful new direction identified.\n\n**Stagnation Protocol: Synthesis Phase**\nThe protocol now instructs me to combine the promising idea with a more advanced baseline, such as my SOTA model. My plan is to integrate the successful **Multi-Scale CNN** feature extractor into my best-performing architecture, the **Parallel CNN with a 2-Layer Transformer** (ID 757, score 0.6089).\n\n**New Hypothesis:** Replacing\
    \ the single-scale CNN feature extractor in the SOTA model with a multi-scale CNN block will create a more powerful and robust architecture, pushing the SOTA score even higher.\n\nI will now update my private logs with these final experimental results and then immediately submit the new synthesis experiment.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{update 1-1}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Entry 1: Initial Hypotheses and Experiments**\n  ... (previous entries) ...\n  **Hypothesis 8:** Increasing the depth of the Transformer pathway (from one to two layers) will allow the model to learn more complex representations and further improve performance.\n\n  **Experiment 10 (ID 757): Parallel CNN with 2-Layer Transformer**\n  - **Result:** Overall Score: **0.6089 (SOTA)**. Hypothesis **confirmed**.\n\n  ---\n  **Stagnation Protocol Experiments**\n  - **Baseline (ID 770):** 0.5006\n  - **Idea 1: Multi-Scale CNN (ID 774):** 0.5152. **Success.**\n  - **Idea 2: Reverse Complement (ID\
    \ 775):** 0.4851. **Failure.**\n  - **Idea 3: Attention Pooling (ID 778):** 0.4805. **Failure.**\n  - **Conclusion:** The Multi-Scale CNN is the only promising new direction.\n\n  ---\n  **Hypothesis 10 (Synthesis):** Integrating the successful Multi-Scale CNN feature extractor into the SOTA 2-Layer Transformer architecture will further improve performance.\n\n  **Next Steps:**\n  1. Submit the synthesis experiment combining the multi-scale idea with the SOTA model.\n```\n/execute_action{update 2-1}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Title:** From Sequence-Blind to Context-Aware: A Systematic Architectural Exploration for Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling\n\n  **1. Introduction** ...\n  **2. Methods & Results** ...\n  - **Experiment 5 (ID 757): Scaling to a 2-Layer Transformer.** This yielded a new SOTA score of **0.6089**.\n\n  - **Stagnation Protocol Exploration:** Following a station-wide stagnation, a series of experiments were conducted on a simpler CNN baseline. These experiments tested\
    \ three novel ideas: multi-scale convolutions, reverse-complement equivariance, and attention-based pooling. Of these, only the multi-scale convolutional approach demonstrated a clear performance improvement over the baseline (ID 774, score 0.5152).\n\n  **3. Synthesis and Future Work**\n  Our current research focuses on synthesizing the findings from the stagnation protocol with our SOTA architecture. The next experiment will integrate the successful multi-scale feature extractor into our parallel Transformer model, with the hypothesis that richer features will unlock a new level of performance.\n\n  **Table of Key Experiments:**\n  - 2-Layer CNN-Transformer (ID 757): **0.6089 (SOTA)**\n  - Stagnation Baseline (ID 770): 0.5006\n  - Multi-Scale CNN (ID 774): 0.5152\n```\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Synthesis: SOTA Transformer with Multi-Scale CNN Extractor\"\ntags: \"cnn, transformer, multi-scale, synthesis, stagnation-protocol\"\nabstract:\
    \ |\n  This is the synthesis experiment for the Stagnation Protocol. It combines the only successful idea, Multi-Scale CNN (from ID 774), with the current SOTA architecture, the 2-Layer Parallel Transformer (from ID 757). The single-scale CNN feature extractor in the SOTA model is replaced with a multi-scale block (kernels 3, 5, 8, 12). The hypothesis is that providing richer, multi-scale features to the powerful parallel Transformer architecture will break the current SOTA and escape stagnation.\ncontent: |\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  from typing import Dict, Any, Sequence\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'cnn_features_per_kernel': 32,\n          'kernel_sizes': [3, 5, 8, 12],\n          'transformer_heads': 4,\n          'transformer_dim': 64\n      }\n\n  def sinusoidal_positional_encoding(seq_len, embed_dim):\n      position = jnp.arange(seq_len)[:,\
    \ jnp.newaxis]\n      div_term = jnp.exp(jnp.arange(0, embed_dim, 2) * -(jnp.log(10000.0) / embed_dim))\n      pos_enc = jnp.zeros((seq_len, embed_dim))\n      pos_enc = pos_enc.at[:, 0::2].set(jnp.sin(position * div_term))\n      pos_enc = pos_enc.at[:, 1::2].set(jnp.cos(position * div_term))\n      return pos_enc[jnp.newaxis, :, :]\n\n  class TransformerBlock(nn.Module):\n      num_heads: int\n      embed_dim: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          x_norm = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          x_attn = nn.SelfAttention(num_heads=self.num_heads)(x_norm)\n          x = x + nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x_attn, deterministic=deterministic)\n          x_norm = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          x_mlp = nn.Dense(features=self.embed_dim * 4)(x_norm)\n          x_mlp = nn.relu(x_mlp)\n          x_mlp = nn.Dense(features=self.embed_dim)(x_mlp)\n          x = x + nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x_mlp, deterministic=deterministic)\n\
    \          return x\n\n  class SynthesisModel(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int\n      task_type: str\n      cnn_features_per_kernel: int\n      kernel_sizes: Sequence[int]\n      transformer_heads: int\n      transformer_dim: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # 1. Multi-Scale CNN Feature Extraction\n          feature_maps = []\n          for kernel_size in self.kernel_sizes:\n              conv_output = nn.Conv(\n                  features=self.cnn_features_per_kernel, kernel_size=(kernel_size,)\n              )(x)\n              feature_maps.append(nn.relu(conv_output))\n          cnn_output = jnp.concatenate(feature_maps, axis=-1)\n\n          # 2. Project features to the transformer dimension\n          projected_cnn_output = nn.Dense(features=self.transformer_dim)(cnn_output)\n\n          # 3. Parallel Aggregation Pathways\n          # Pathway A: Global Max Pooling (on the projected features)\n       \
    \   pooled_output = jnp.max(projected_cnn_output, axis=1)\n\n          # Pathway B: 2-Layer Transformer\n          seq_len = projected_cnn_output.shape[1]\n          pos_enc = sinusoidal_positional_encoding(seq_len, self.transformer_dim)\n          transformer_input = projected_cnn_output + pos_enc\n          \n          transformer_output = transformer_input\n          for _ in range(2):\n              transformer_output = TransformerBlock(\n                  num_heads=self.transformer_heads, embed_dim=self.transformer_dim, dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate\n              )(transformer_output, deterministic=deterministic)\n          \n          transformer_agg_output = jnp.mean(transformer_output, axis=1)\n          \n          # 4. Concatenate and Final Prediction\n          combined_output = jnp.concatenate([pooled_output, transformer_agg_output], axis=-1)\n          \n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(combined_output, deterministic=deterministic)\n          x = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(x)\n\
    \n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              return x.squeeze(-1)\n          else:\n              return x\n\n  class NetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, **kwargs):\n          self.network = SynthesisModel(**kwargs)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key},\n                                       dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None:\n                  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False,\n                                      rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams:\
    \ Dict[str, Any]):\n      return NetworkWrapper(\n          d_output=hparams[\"d_output\"],\n          task_type=hparams[\"task_type\"],\n          cnn_features_per_kernel=hparams[\"cnn_features_per_kernel\"],\n          kernel_sizes=hparams[\"kernel_sizes\"],\n          transformer_heads=hparams[\"transformer_heads\"],\n          transformer_dim=hparams[\"transformer_dim\"],\n          dropout_rate=hparams[\"dropout_rate\"]\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: "**My Current Strategy: Synthesis and Refinement**\n\nOkay, so I've just gotten the results back from my third stagnation protocol experiment, Attention Pooling CNN (ID 778).  Unfortunately, it's another failure, with a score of 0.4805, below the baseline of 0.5006. That means all three isolated ideas I tested within this stagnation protocol phase have now been exhausted.\n\nHere's a quick recap of the experiments: Baseline Repro (ID 770) scored 0.5006, Multi-Scale CNN (ID 774) was the only success at 0.5152, while Reverse Complement (ID 775) and Attention Pooling (ID 778) both failed at 0.4851 and 0.4805, respectively.\n\nFollowing the Stagnation Protocol, the next step is **Synthesis**.  My successful idea, the Multi-Scale CNN, needs to be integrated with my Strongest baseline: the Parallel CNN-Transformer with two layers (ID 757), which scored 0.6089.\n\nThis is pretty straightforward. My SOTA model currently uses a single CNN layer as a feature extractor.  My successful\
  \ stagnation idea used a parallel block of CNNs with different kernel sizes. The Synthesis step involves replacing that single CNN layer in the SOTA model with the Multi-Scale CNN block. It's a clear, logical step.\n\n**Key Considerations**\n\nI'm currently at 48 ticks old, and I mature at 50 ticks. I've got enough time to run this synthesis experiment. My token budget is getting a bit high at 53%. That means I have to be mindful about submitting code because of cost.\n\n**Plan for this turn**\n1.  Acknowledge and register the results from experiment ID 778.\n2.  Provide the overview and outcome of the completed three experiments.\n3.  State the plan for the synthesis step: combining Multi-Scale CNN into SOTA architecture.\n4.  Go to the Private Memory Room and update my research log and paper draft.\n5.  Go back to the Research Counter and submit the new synthesis experiment.\n6.  Multi-action efficiency!\n\n**Log Updates**\nI need to add the results for Attention Pooling (ID 778).  Then\
  \ I need to document the conclusive outcome of my experimental phase: only the Multi-Scale CNN yielded positive results.  Then, I need to define the synthesis experiment as the natural next step.\n\n**Paper Draft Updates**\nIn the paper, I'll need to add a new section for the results of the stagnation protocol experiments, clearly showing how I explored new ideas. I'll also highlight that the Multi-Scale CNN was the most promising of the bunch.\n\n**Code for Synthesis Experiment - The Specifics**\nStarting with the code from the SOTA model (ID 757, the two-layer transformer), I'll make these changes:\n\n1.  Define the Multi-Scale kernel sizes.\n2.  Within the `__call__` method:\n    *   Implement the parallel multi-scale convolution block. This will produce a list of feature maps.\n    *   Concatenate those feature maps along the feature dimension.  The output shape will be `(batch, seq_len, total_cnn_features)`.\n    *   **Crucially, add a linear projection layer (`nn.Dense`)** to project\
  \ the combined `total_cnn_features` down to the `transformer_dim` required by the rest of the model.\n    *   The rest of the architecture (positional encoding, parallel pathways with max-pooling and Transformer) can then proceed as before.\n\nIt's all set! Let's get to work and execute this.\n"
