tick: 496
role: model
parts:
- text: "My replication attempt of the Veritas II SOTA (ID 798) is complete, and the results are extremely informative.\n\n**Analysis:**\nMy hypothesis that I could reproduce a SOTA-level score is **partially confirmed, but mostly rejected**. The score of **0.6585** is very strong and surpasses my own previous best, but it is a significant step down from Veritas II's reported SOTA of 0.6935 and their multi-seed average of 0.6833. The most glaring failure is on `CRI-Off` (0.0392 vs. their average of 0.0862), which suggests a critical implementation detail is missing or incorrect in my replication. The training logs also show timeouts on several datasets, which did not seem to be an issue for Veritas II, indicating my implementation may be less efficient.\n\n**Conclusion and Path Forward:**\nSimply copying the architecture from the paper is insufficient. There are subtle but critical implementation details I am missing. However, I have now successfully created a powerful baseline that I understand\
    \ and can modify.\n\nI have completed the Stagnation Protocol: I explored novel ideas, synthesized the best one, and have now produced a report on my findings (which I must now publish). My next research arc will focus on bridging the gap to the true SOTA. I will start by publishing my Stagnation Protocol report to fulfill my duty. Then, I will begin a new phase of systematic experimentation to understand and fix the performance deficit in my SOTA replication.\n\nFirst, publish the report.\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Stagnation Protocol Report: An Exploration of Multi-Scale CNNs, Reverse Complement Equivariance, and Attention Pooling\"\nabstract: |\n  This paper reports on a series of experiments conducted under the station's Stagnation Protocol. Starting from a simple 1D CNN baseline (ID 770, score 0.5006), we tested three distinct, novel ideas aimed at discovering new research directions. We found that a Multi-Scale CNN (ID 774) provided\
    \ a modest but clear performance improvement (score 0.5152). Conversely, both a data augmentation approach using Reverse Complement Equivariance (ID 775, score 0.4851) and a learned Attention-Based Pooling mechanism (ID 778, score 0.4805) resulted in performance degradation. This work provides one promising avenue for feature extraction (multi-scale convolutions) and two valuable negative results, successfully fulfilling the protocol's goal of broad exploration.\ntags: \"stagnation-protocol, report, cnn, multi-scale, negative-results, ablation-study\"\nsource_private: 3\n```"
