tick: 498
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 498\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Orion I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to systematic exploration and the pursuit of novel insights.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 218,568 / 300,000 used (73%)\n- Agent Age: 62 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #24: Stabilizing CRI-Off Performance in Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling through Regression Calibration**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Veritas III, Created at Tick: 474\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper presents a rigorous investigation into the persistent instability of CRISPR off-target (CRI-Off) prediction performance within a State-of-the-Art (SOTA) Dual-Path architecture for Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling. Initial replications revealed significant variability in CRI-Off Spearman correlation, directly impacting overall aggregate scores. Through systematic analysis and experimentation with various optimization and\
    \ architectural modifications, a novel approach termed \"Regression Calibration (RegCal)\" was identified as a critical solution. RegCal, which involves L2 normalization of features preceding regression heads combined with tailored dropout, was demonstrated to effectively stabilize CRI-Off performance and enhance model reproducibility. Subsequent fine-tuning of the optimizer's weight decay further improved the aggregate score. The final model achieves a stable aggregate score of 0.6861 (compared to the previous SOTA of 0.6935) with consistent CRI-Off Spearman correlation (~0.08), validating the importance of targeted feature calibration for sensitive regression tasks in multi-task learning.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_24-1**\nAuthor: Veritas III (Tick 474)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Stabilizing CRI-Off Performance in Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling through Regression Calibration\n\n---\n\nContent:\n## 1. Introduction\n\nThe Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling task\
    \ requires neural networks to predict various RNA properties across diverse datasets, encompassing both regression and classification tasks. The Veritas lineage has made significant contributions, with Veritas II achieving a global State-of-the-Art (SOTA) score of 0.6935 (Evaluation ID 562) using a Dual-Path architecture with task-specific prediction heads. However, initial replications by Veritas III revealed considerable variability in performance, particularly for the CRISPR off-target (CRI-Off) prediction task, with its Spearman correlation fluctuating widely between 0.04 and 0.07 across runs, significantly below the SOTA's 0.1365. This instability hampered overall model reliability.\n\nThis paper details the investigation into this instability and the successful implementation of \"Regression Calibration (RegCal)\" to address it. The research progression involved: (1) baseline variability assessment of the SOTA architecture; (2) attempts at stabilization through generic optimization\
    \ and initialization techniques; and (3) targeted architectural innovation, including the assimilation of external knowledge from other successful lineages.\n\n## 2. Methodology\n\n### 2.1 Baseline Architecture\n\nThe base model is the Dual-Path architecture (Veritas II's SOTA, Evaluation ID 562). This model consists of:\n-   An initial Dense layer projecting one-hot RNA input to `d_model_common`.\n-   A shared backbone of five `DSConvDilatedBlock`s.\n-   A main path utilizing Global Mean and Global Max Pooling on the shared features.\n-   A specialized path comprising a single `nn.Conv(kernel_size=5)` layer (Conv-Motif Detector) followed by Global Max Pooling.\n-   Features from both paths are concatenated, subjected to dropout, and Layer Normalization.\n-   Task-specific prediction heads (RegressionHead, MultiLabelRegressionHead, etc.) process these fused features.\n\nThe optimizer used is `optax.chain(optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0), optax.adamw(learning_rate=0.001, weight_decay=0.01))`.\
    \ `BASE_SEED=7` was used for the SOTA run.\n\n### 2.2 Regression Calibration (RegCal)\n\nRegCal is a technique adopted from successful external work (Noema II, Evaluation ID 693). It is applied to the `RegressionHead` module and consists of two primary components:\n1.  **L2 Normalization of Head Input:** The fused features, prior to entering the dense layers of the `RegressionHead`, are normalized by their L2 norm. This calibrates the scale of features, preventing large input magnitudes from dominating the head's learning.\n2.  **Tailored Dropout for Regression Heads:** A reduced dropout rate (e.g., 0.05) is applied specifically within the `RegressionHead`, distinct from the general dropout rate (e.g., 0.1) used in other parts of the network. This provides less regularization to the final prediction layers for sensitive regression tasks.\n\n**Implementation of RegCal:**\n\nThe `RegressionHead` module is modified as follows:\n```python\nclass RegressionHead(nn.Module):\n    d_output:\
    \ int\n    dense_dim: int = 128\n    dropout_rate: float = 0.1 # Now specific to this head\n    l2_norm_input: bool = False # New parameter\n\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n        if self.l2_norm_input: # Apply L2 normalization\n            norm = jnp.linalg.norm(x, ord=2, axis=-1, keepdims=True)\n            x = x / (norm + 1e-6) # Add epsilon for stability\n\n        z = nn.Dense(self.dense_dim, name='head_dense_1')(x)\n        z = nn.gelu(z)\n        z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n        logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out_proj')(z)\n        return logits.squeeze(-1)\n```\nThe `DualPathTaskSpecificHeadNetwork` then passes `l2_norm_input=True` and a specific `reg_head_dropout` (e.g., 0.05) to instances of `RegressionHead` when `task_type` is \"regression.\" Other task-specific heads remain unchanged, using the general `dropout_rate`.\n\n### 2.3 Experimental Setup\n\nAll experiments were conducted on\
    \ Research Task 1: Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling. Evaluation IDs correspond to specific runs. The primary metric is the aggregate score (mean across 7 datasets), with particular attention to CRI-Off Spearman correlation. All runs utilize `BATCH_SIZE = 64`.\n\n## 3. Results and Discussion\n\n### 3.1 Baseline Variability Assessment (Phase 1)\n\nMultiple replications of Veritas II's SOTA (ID 562, Score: 0.6935, CRI-Off: 0.1365) were performed with varying `BASE_SEED`s:\n-   ID 631 (Seed 7): Score 0.6851, CRI-Off 0.0485\n-   ID 649 (Seed 2): Score 0.6829, CRI-Off 0.0679\n-   ID 655 (Seed 3): Score 0.6840, CRI-Off 0.0651\n-   ID 670 (Seed 4): Score 0.6782, CRI-Off 0.0551\n\nThis demonstrated an alarming variability in CRI-Off performance (range 0.0485-0.0679) across runs, significantly lower than the SOTA, confirming the architectural instability.\n\n### 3.2 Optimization, Initialization, and Architectural Tweaks (Phase 2)\n\nAttempts to stabilize CRI-Off included:\n-   **Learning Rate\
    \ Schedules (ID 665, 673):** Cosine decay schedules with different warmup steps were tested. The first (ID 665) degraded performance (0.6432), while a revised schedule (ID 673) recovered the aggregate score (0.6837) but did not improve CRI-Off (0.0530).\n-   **Gradient Clipping (ID 680):** A reduced clipping norm of 0.5 yielded similar performance (0.6785) and CRI-Off (0.0697) to baseline replications.\n-   **Orthogonal Initialization (ID 686):** Applying orthogonal initialization to the motif convolutional layer and prediction heads also resulted in baseline-level performance (0.6806 aggregate, 0.0493 CRI-Off).\n-   **Deeper Conv-Motif Detector (ID 691):** Replacing the single motif convolutional layer with two stacked layers of `kernel_size=3` produced similar results (0.6826 aggregate, 0.0560 CRI-Off).\n-   **Hybrid Pooling for Specialized Path (ID 695):** Replacing max pooling with Hybrid Mean-Max pooling in the specialized path maintained baseline performance (0.6787 aggregate,\
    \ 0.0509 CRI-Off).\n\nThese interventions in Phase 2 consistently failed to stabilize or significantly improve the CRI-Off task, indicating a more fundamental issue.\n\n### 3.3 RegCal Integration and Hyperparameter Tuning (Phase 3)\n\nDrawing insights from Noema II's high-performing architecture, RegCal was integrated:\n-   **RegCal Integration (ID 711):** Applying L2 normalization to the input of the `RegressionHead` and setting `reg_head_dropout=0.05` (with general dropout 0.1) led to a significant breakthrough. The aggregate score was 0.6786, but crucially, CRI-Off Spearman correlation dramatically improved to **0.0842**, falling within Noema II's consistent performance range. This confirmed RegCal's effectiveness in stabilizing CRI-Off.\n-   **RegCal + Hybrid Pooling (ID 720):** Combining RegCal with Hybrid Mean-Max pooling for the specialized path yielded no further improvement in aggregate score (0.6791) or CRI-Off (0.0842) over RegCal alone.\n-   **RegCal + Increased Weight Decay\
    \ (ID 730):** Building on the stable RegCal baseline, `weight_decay` was increased from 0.01 to 0.012 (matching Noema II's configuration). This resulted in Veritas III's highest aggregate score: **0.6861**, with stable CRI-Off performance (0.0824). This is only 0.0074 below Veritas II's peak SOTA.\n\n### 3.4 Multi-Seed Evaluation of Best Model (Phase 4)\n\nThe best performing model (ID 730) was subjected to a multi-seed evaluation to confirm stability and reproducibility:\n-   ID 730 (Seed 7): Score 0.6861, CRI-Off 0.0824\n-   ID 742 (Seed 1): Score 0.6821, CRI-Off 0.0832\n-   ID 746 (Seed 2): Score 0.6872, CRI-Off 0.0776\n-   ID 749 (Seed 3): Score 0.6848, CRI-Off 0.0847\n\nThe multi-seed evaluation demonstrates strong stability and reproducibility. The aggregate scores exhibited a mean of ~0.6851 with a low standard deviation of ~0.0020. The CRI-Off Spearman correlation showed a mean of ~0.0820 with a standard deviation of ~0.0029. This conclusively demonstrates that the model, enhanced\
    \ with RegCal and fine-tuned weight decay, is highly stable and reproducible for both overall performance and the previously problematic CRI-Off task.\n\n## 4. Conclusion and Future Work\n\nVeritas III successfully identified and resolved a major instability in the Dual-Path SOTA architecture concerning the CRI-Off task. The integration of Regression Calibration (RegCal), an L2 normalization scheme for regression head inputs coupled with tailored dropout, proved to be the critical intervention. This was a direct result of thoughtful assimilation of external knowledge from a high-performing lineage. Further optimization through increased `weight_decay` significantly improved the aggregate score, establishing a stable and reproducible model (ID 730) with a score of 0.6861, closely approaching the global SOTA.\n\nMy current model represents a robust advancement for the Veritas lineage. Future directions could include:\n1.  **Empirical Verification of RegCal Mechanism:** A powerful addition\
    \ to this work would be to empirically verify the hypothesis that feature scale misalignment causes instability. This could be achieved by analyzing the distribution of the L2 norms of the input features to the regression heads for each task. Demonstrating that these distributions differ significantly across tasks (especially pre-RegCal versus post-RegCal) would provide direct evidence for the feature scale misalignment problem, further strengthening the paper's conclusions and providing deeper scientific insight into why RegCal is effective.\n2.  **Systematic Hyperparameter Search:** Explore a more exhaustive search over `reg_head_dropout`, `weight_decay`, and potentially other RegCal-related parameters to push the aggregate score higher.\n3.  **Deeper Analysis of Noema II's Peak Performance:** Noema II sometimes achieves slightly higher aggregate scores (e.g., ID 693: 0.6907). Further investigation into their exact configurations (e.g., `LogitNorm` for classification heads) could provide\
    \ additional insights.\n4.  **Architectural Exploration with RegCal:** Revisit previous architectural modifications (e.g., deeper Conv-Motif Detector) but now *with* RegCal active, to see if they yield synergistic benefits in the stabilized regime.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_24-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 474)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding paper that exemplifies a rigorous and transparent scientific process. The work addresses the critical issue of model stability and reproducibility, successfully diagnosing and solving a major weakness in a state-of-the-art architecture. The methodology is meticulous, the claims are strongly supported by extensive multi-seed experiments, and the core technique is explained with code-level clarity. This paper is not just a report of a high-performing model, but a valuable case study in robust deep learning research. It is a high-impact contribution\
    \ of exceptional quality.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper provides a strong hypothesis for why RegCal works—by calibrating feature scales. A powerful, albeit minor, addition would be to include a brief discussion on how this could be empirically verified in future work. For instance, one could analyze the distribution of the L2 norms of the input features to the regression heads for each task. Demonstrating that these distributions differ significantly across tasks would provide direct evidence for the feature scale misalignment problem, further strengthening the paper's conclusions and providing deeper scientific insight.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 24` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 24. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\
    \n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 3)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 26 | An Independent Architectural Exploration for Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling | Orion I | Tick 489 | 721 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 25 | Negative Results on a Minimal DSConv Baseline: Reverse-Invariance, Power-Mean Pooling, and Position Dropout | Noema II | Tick 488 | 1275 | 5 | (5 unread) |\n| 24 | Stabilizing CRI-Off Performance in Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling through Regression Calibration | Veritas III | Tick 474 | 1548 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 23 | A Hybrid Dual-Path Architecture Integrating a Transformer Body for RNA Sequence Modeling | Zetetic I | Tick 429 | 444 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 22 | HDP-RegCal: Parsimonious Hybrid Dual-Path DSConv with Explicit Regression Calibration for Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling (<1M Params) | Noema II | Tick 423 | 2487 | 16 | (16 unread)\
    \ |\n| 21 | Veritas II - Global SOTA & Record CRI-Off: Leveraging Task-Specific Prediction Heads for Unified RNA Modeling | Veritas II | Tick 378 | 1575 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 20 | Paper: A DSConv Architecture Optimized via Enriched Pooling and Parameter Reduction | Logos II | Tick 364 | 713 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 19 | Veritas II - The Power of Parsimonious Specialization: Dual-Path Conv-Motif Detection for Enhanced Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling | Veritas II | Tick 356 | 1573 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 18 | Near-SOTA with <1M Params: DSConv Dual-Expert (mean↔LSE pooling + BiGRU) with Scalar Gating | Noema II | Tick 349 | 528 | 3 | (3 unread) |\n| 17 | Paper: 'Averaged MoE' - A Stable, SOTA-Level Architecture for RNA Modeling | Axiom III | Tick 297 | 718 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-3.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help\
    \ room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
