role: model
parts:
- text: "I have thoroughly read the paper on Regression Calibration by Veritas III (Archive #24). This is a critical piece of the puzzle. My SOTA replication (ID 798) failed to reproduce the SOTA score precisely because it suffered from the exact problem this paper solves: a massive performance drop on `CRI-Off`.\n\nThe solution, \"RegCal,\" is simple, elegant, and clearly explained. It involves two components applied only to the regression heads:\n1.  L2 normalization of the input features.\n2.  A tailored, lower dropout rate.\n\nVeritas III also noted that a slightly increased `weight_decay` (from 0.01 to 0.012) provided a further boost.\n\n**New Plan: Correct the Replication**\n\nMy next step is clear. I must correct my SOTA replication to include these crucial details. I will not add any of my own novel components yet. My priority is to first create a stable, high-performing baseline that I fully understand.\n\n**New Hypothesis:** By applying Regression Calibration (L2 norm + tailored\
    \ dropout) and the optimized weight decay to my SOTA replication model (ID 798), I will be able to resolve the `CRI-Off` performance deficit and achieve a score in the true SOTA range (≈0.685).\n\nI will now go to the Research Counter and submit this corrected replication.\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"SOTA Replication + RegCal & Optimized Weight Decay\"\ntags: \"replication, sota, dsconv, dual-path, regcal, stabilization\"\nabstract: |\n  This submission corrects my previous SOTA replication attempt (ID 798), which suffered from poor CRI-Off performance. Based on a detailed review of Veritas III's paper (Archive #24), I am now incorporating their \"Regression Calibration\" (RegCal) technique—L2 normalization and a tailored 0.05 dropout for regression heads—and their optimized weight decay of 0.012. The hypothesis is that these crucial details will stabilize the model, resolve the CRI-Off performance gap, and successfully reproduce a\
    \ score in the true SOTA range.\ncontent: |\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n  from typing import Dict, Any, Sequence\n\n  # Sourced from storage/veritas/utils.py\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dilation: int = 1\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1 \n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\n              feature_group_count=self.d_model, padding='SAME',\n              kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,), name='dw_conv'\n          )(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,), name='pw_conv')(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  # My implementation of the Task-Specific Heads, now with RegCal\n\
    \  class TaskHead(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int\n      task_type: str\n      dense_dim: int = 128\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      reg_head_dropout: float = 0.05\n      apply_regcal: bool = True\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          head_dropout = self.reg_head_dropout if self.task_type == 'regression' else self.dropout_rate\n          \n          if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.apply_regcal:\n              norm = jnp.linalg.norm(x, ord=2, axis=-1, keepdims=True)\n              x = x / (norm + 1e-6)\n\n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_dim)(x)\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=head_dropout)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(z)\n          \n          if self.task_type == 'regression':\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  class SotaReproductionWithRegCal(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int\n      task_type: str\n      \n      d_model:\
    \ int = 256\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dilations: Sequence[int] = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16)\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      motif_detector_kernel_size: int = 5\n      motif_detector_features: int = 256\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          x = nn.Dense(features=self.d_model)(x)\n          \n          dsconv_features = x\n          for dilation in self.dilations:\n              dsconv_features = DSConvDilatedBlock(\n                  d_model=self.d_model, kernel_size=self.kernel_size,\n                  dilation=dilation, dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate\n              )(dsconv_features, deterministic=deterministic)\n          \n          motif_features = nn.Conv(\n              features=self.motif_detector_features,\n              kernel_size=(self.motif_detector_kernel_size,)\n          )(x)\n          motif_features = nn.gelu(motif_features)\n          \n          main_agg = jnp.concatenate([\n              jnp.mean(dsconv_features,\
    \ axis=1), jnp.max(dsconv_features, axis=1)\n          ], axis=-1)\n          motif_agg = jnp.max(motif_features, axis=1)\n\n          combined_features = jnp.concatenate([main_agg, motif_agg], axis=-1)\n          fused_features = nn.LayerNorm()(combined_features)\n          \n          y = TaskHead(d_output=self.d_output, task_type=self.task_type)(fused_features, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return y\n\n  class NetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, **kwargs):\n          self.network = SotaReproductionWithRegCal(**kwargs)\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key},\n                                       dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params},\
    \ x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None: rng_key = random.PRNGKey(0)\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False,\n                                      rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {'learning_rate': 0.001}\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return NetworkWrapper(d_output=hparams[\"d_output\"], task_type=hparams[\"task_type\"])\n      \n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001) -> optax.GradientTransformation:\n      # Using the optimized weight decay from Veritas III's paper\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.012)\n      )\n```"
