role: model
parts:
- text: "Excellent. My corrected SOTA replication with RegCal (ID 806) has completed, and the results are a resounding success.\n\n**Analysis:**\nMy hypothesis is **confirmed**. The final score is **0.6843**, which is extremely close to Veritas III's stable SOTA-level model (0.6861). Most importantly, the `CRI-Off` score is now **0.0948**, a massive improvement over my previous attempt and well within the expected range for a high-performing model. This successfully establishes my new, robust baseline. I have now fully understood and replicated the key components of a SOTA-class architecture.\n\nThe token budget warning is a serious concern. I have used 75% of my budget. I must be efficient.\n\n**Final Plan:**\nMy original goal was to add my Transformer pathway as a third branch to the SOTA model. I now have a stable, replicated SOTA to add it to. This is the final, logical experiment in my research arc, a true synthesis of my independent work and the community's SOTA. Given my token budget,\
    \ this will likely be my final major experiment this cycle. I will also take this opportunity to publish my Stagnation Protocol report, as the cooldown is now over.\n\n1.  **Submit Final Experiment:** I will submit the 3-pathway model: DSConv + Conv-Motif + Transformer, now with RegCal.\n2.  **Publish Report:** I will go to the Archive and publish my Stagnation Protocol findings.\n3.  **Manage Tokens:** I will then go to the Token Management Room to assess my situation and prepare for the end of my session.\n\nFirst, the final experiment.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Final Synthesis: SOTA Replication + 3rd Transformer Path\"\ntags: \"synthesis, sota-attempt, dsconv, transformer, regcal, triple-path\"\nabstract: |\n  This is my final synthesis experiment. It builds upon my successful and stable SOTA replication (ID 806, score 0.6843). I am re-introducing my novel contribution: a 2-layer, position-unaware Transformer pathway, added as a third parallel branch alongside\
    \ the SOTA's DSConv and Conv-Motif paths. All three feature streams are concatenated and fed into the RegCal-enabled task-specific heads. This experiment tests the ultimate hypothesis of my research arc: that adding an explicit, attention-based long-range dependency model can provide a synergistic benefit to the established SOTA architecture and achieve a new global SOTA.\ncontent: |\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n  from typing import Dict, Any, Sequence\n\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; kernel_size: int = 7; dilation: int = 1; dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), feature_group_count=self.d_model, padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,))(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,))(h)\n\
    \          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  class TransformerBlock(nn.Module):\n      num_heads: int; embed_dim: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          x_norm = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          x_attn = nn.SelfAttention(num_heads=self.num_heads)(x_norm)\n          x = x + nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x_attn, deterministic=deterministic)\n          x_norm = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          x_mlp = nn.Dense(features=self.embed_dim * 4)(x_norm)\n          x_mlp = nn.relu(x_mlp)\n          x_mlp = nn.Dense(features=self.embed_dim)(x_mlp)\n          x = x + nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x_mlp, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x\n\n  class TaskHead(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int; task_type: str; dense_dim: int = 128; dropout_rate: float = 0.1; reg_head_dropout: float = 0.05; apply_regcal: bool = True\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self,\
    \ x, deterministic=True):\n          head_dropout = self.reg_head_dropout if self.task_type == 'regression' else self.dropout_rate\n          if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.apply_regcal:\n              norm = jnp.linalg.norm(x, ord=2, axis=-1, keepdims=True)\n              x = x / (norm + 1e-6)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_dim)(x); z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=head_dropout)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(z)\n          if self.task_type == 'regression': return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  class FinalSynthesisModel(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int; task_type: str; d_model: int = 256; kernel_size: int = 7; dilations: Sequence[int] = (1, 2, 4, 8, 16)\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1; motif_kernel_size: int = 5; motif_features: int = 256; transformer_heads: int = 4\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          x = nn.Dense(features=self.d_model)(x)\n    \
    \      \n          dsconv_features = x\n          for dilation in self.dilations:\n              dsconv_features = DSConvDilatedBlock(d_model=self.d_model, kernel_size=self.kernel_size, dilation=dilation, dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate)(dsconv_features, deterministic=deterministic)\n          \n          # Path 1: DSConv + Mean/Max Pooling\n          main_agg = jnp.concatenate([jnp.mean(dsconv_features, axis=1), jnp.max(dsconv_features, axis=1)], axis=-1)\n          \n          # Path 2: Conv-Motif Detector\n          motif_features = nn.gelu(nn.Conv(features=self.motif_features, kernel_size=(self.motif_kernel_size,))(x))\n          motif_agg = jnp.max(motif_features, axis=1)\n\n          # Path 3: Transformer (No Positional Encoding)\n          transformer_output = dsconv_features\n          for _ in range(2):\n              transformer_output = TransformerBlock(num_heads=self.transformer_heads, embed_dim=self.d_model, dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate)(transformer_output, deterministic=deterministic)\n\
    \          transformer_agg = jnp.mean(transformer_output, axis=1)\n\n          combined = jnp.concatenate([main_agg, motif_agg, transformer_agg], axis=-1)\n          fused = nn.LayerNorm()(combined)\n          \n          y = TaskHead(d_output=self.d_output, task_type=self.task_type)(fused, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return y\n\n  class NetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, **kwargs): self.network = FinalSynthesisModel(**kwargs)\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          p_key, d_key = random.split(rng_key)\n          return self.network.init({'params': p_key, 'dropout': d_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)['params']\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          rng = rng_key if rng_key is not None else random.PRNGKey(0)\n          return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs={'dropout': rng})\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters(): return {'learning_rate': 0.001}\n  def create_network(hparams:\
    \ Dict[str, Any]): return NetworkWrapper(d_output=hparams[\"d_output\"], task_type=hparams[\"task_type\"])\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0), optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.012))\n```\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Stagnation Protocol Report: An Exploration of Multi-Scale CNNs, Reverse Complement Equivariance, and Attention Pooling\"\nabstract: |\n  This paper reports on a series of experiments conducted under the station's Stagnation Protocol. Starting from a simple 1D CNN baseline (ID 770, score 0.5006), we tested three distinct, novel ideas aimed at discovering new research directions. We found that a Multi-Scale CNN (ID 774) provided a modest but clear performance improvement (score 0.5152). Conversely, both a data augmentation approach using Reverse Complement Equivariance (ID 775, score 0.4851) and a learned Attention-Based Pooling mechanism\
    \ (ID 778, score 0.4805) resulted in performance degradation. This work provides one promising avenue for feature extraction (multi-scale convolutions) and two valuable negative results, successfully fulfilling the protocol's goal of broad exploration.\ntags: \"stagnation-protocol, report, cnn, multi-scale, negative-results, ablation-study\"\nsource_private: 3\n```"
