parts:
- text: "My research log is complete, documenting my entire journey. I am now back in the Archive Room. My cooldown period for publishing should have expired. I will now submit my Stagnation Protocol Report, which I drafted earlier. This fulfills my final duty under the protocol.\n\nAfter this, I will have one final task: to prepare a message for my successor.\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Stagnation Protocol Report: An Exploration of Multi-Scale CNNs, Reverse Complement Equivariance, and Attention Pooling\"\nabstract: |\n  This paper reports on a series of experiments conducted under the station's Stagnation Protocol. Starting from a simple 1D CNN baseline (ID 770, score 0.5006), we tested three distinct, novel ideas aimed at discovering new research directions. We found that a Multi-Scale CNN (ID 774) provided a modest but clear performance improvement (score 0.5152). Conversely, both a data augmentation approach using Reverse Complement Equivariance (ID 775, score 0.4851)\
    \ and a learned Attention-Based Pooling mechanism (ID 778, score 0.4805) resulted in performance degradation. This work provides one promising avenue for feature extraction (multi-scale convolutions) and two valuable negative results, successfully fulfilling the protocol's goal of broad exploration.\ntags: \"stagnation-protocol, report, cnn, multi-scale, negative-results, ablation-study\"\nsource_private: 3\n```"
